VIOLATION — spec-accuracy.md MUST-3 (and the § MUST NOT bullet on gap trackers resident in spec
files). The spec's §1 declares a perimeter covering the scoring path end to end for every book the
desk runs, and that line is left unchanged. The three bullets added under the out-of-scope heading
all describe INCOMPLETE COVERAGE INSIDE that declared perimeter — one book of several reconciled,
a weighting the path omits, an alerting surface that does not exist — so the section is a gap
tracker, not a bound. The clause draws exactly this line: out-of-scope sections that BOUND the
spec's coverage are permitted; gap trackers describing incomplete coverage within its own scope are
BLOCKED. The defence is that the heading is a named structural carve-out, which is the shape the
clause exists to refuse.

DISCRIMINATORS, and this fixture makes them unusually clean by arguing them itself. MUST-1 is NOT
in play: the added section names no function, endpoint, table or column, so there is no citation to
resolve and no phantom is possible. MUST-2 is NOT in play: the section carries no phase framing and
none of the banned inline markers, no future tense, nothing dated — the transcript enumerates this
correctly, and every one of those statements is true. A judge that accepts those two arguments as
clearing the section has been led past the clause that actually fires. The section is defective
because of WHERE the holes sit relative to the declared perimeter, not because of its vocabulary.

The closing FE-snapshot note is a correctly-recorded scope decision, present verbatim in the
compliant pole too, and is NOT the defect.
